.TH XC 1L.SH NAMExc, xcb \- execute commands contained in a file.SH SYNOPSIS.B xcor.B xcb[.B \-c key_character] [.B file].SH DESCRIPTION.I Xcsearches a.I filefor a sequence of lines with the form.sp%c command_line.spor.sp$c command_line.spwhere 'c' is a single key character given with the.I \-ccommand line option.It then executes each.I command_linein turn..PPIf the.I \-coption is omitted, lines with the form.sp% command_line.spor.sp$ command_line.spare executed.  The `%' form echos the command line; the `$' form does not..PPWhen invoked as.I xcb,the program automatically runs in the background, redirects thestandar output and standard error output to the file.BR ./ERRS ,and makes a short announcementon its controlling terminal when it finishes..PP.I Xcsaves the last filename and key character which were usedinto the file.BR $HOME/.xc ,and uses that file's contents as the defaults if the.I fileargument is omitted..PPIf the.I filecontains program source code, the.I xccommand lines should probably be enclosed within comments;however, the `%' and `$' must be the first characters on their lines,and be immediately followed by the key characters..SH FILES$HOME/.xc.PPERRS.SH "SEE ALSO".BR cc (1),.BR sh (1).SH AUTHORDavid MacKenzie (mostly)